{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NNSRbPohb53RQ6MKhJk884","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nNow I have all the context needed. Let me carefully analyze the discrepancies between the different number sets provided in the task prompt versus the paper.tex, and write a thorough R1 review based on the numbers explicitly given in the task prompt (which represent the paper being reviewed).\n\nKey observation: The task prompt numbers (57.1% DCOL 6-class, 82.3% 4-class, CORAL at 53.8%) are substantially lower than the \"best\" dream paper.tex numbers (72.2%, etc.). The task prompt also mentions LOKI and a \"binary cascade\" baseline not in the paper.tex, and references Tables up to Table 16. This appears to be a different (more realistic) version of the paper results. I will review the paper as described in the task prompt.\n\n## R1 Review: Methods & Experiments\n\n### Summary\nThis paper formalizes ordinal generation depth estimation -- predicting the number of recursive generation cycles (d in {0,...,5}) a text has undergone -- and proposes DCOL, a DeBERTa-v3-large method with contrastive ordinal loss incorporating learnable non-uniform margins, surprisal-augmented anchor selection, and strategy conditioning. The paper also contributes a Depth Degradation Atlas characterizing feature evolution across 3 models x 3 decoding strategies x 6 depths, and a proof-of-concept showing depth-graduated curation outperforms binary filtering. DCOL achieves 57.1% 6-class accuracy (chance 16.7%) and 82.3% 4-class accuracy, outperforming the best baseline (CORAL) by +3.3pp on 6-class. The experimental coverage is remarkably comprehensive, spanning 11 subsections with per-model benchmarks, generalization experiments, ablations, extended depth analysis, granularity studies, wild-data calibration, and downstream application.\n\n### Strengths (Methods)\n\n- **Well-motivated design loop.** The Atlas documents non-uniform inter-depth geometry (d0->1 gap is 2.83x the d2->3 gap), which directly motivates the learnable non-uniform margins in DCOL. The learned margin ratios closely mirror empirical distances (reported Pearson r > 0.95). This observation-to-design-to-validation chain is methodologically clean.\n\n- **Ordinal inductive bias is principled.** The ordinal regression head mapping to cumulative probabilities P(D>d) with threshold at 0.5 is a well-established approach (extending CORAL). The monotonicity constraint on margins (m_1 >= m_2 >= ... >= m_K > 0) is a sensible structural prior matching the documented decay in inter-depth distances.\n\n- **Feature engineering is well-grounded.** The 19-dimensional statistical feature vector covering surprisal statistics, perplexity statistics, lexical diversity, and distributional measures is comprehensive. The identification of surprisal variance as the strongest depth signal (monotonicity 0.847) has standalone scientific value and is theoretically motivated by the variance compression inherent in recursive sampling.\n\n- **Strategy conditioning addresses a real problem.** Different decoding strategies produce different depth boundary locations (EDD ranges from 2 for OLMo+temperature to 4 for GPT2XL+nucleus). The lightweight strategy embedding modulation is an appropriate solution.\n\n### Strengths (Experiments)\n\n- **Exceptional experimental breadth.** Eleven experiment subsections covering per-model benchmarks (Tables 1-3), full baseline comparison (Table 4), E2E comparison (Table 5), ablation (Table 6), EDD analysis (Table 7), feature monotonicity (Table 8), extended depth (Table 9), granularity (Table 10), cross-model LOMO (Table 11), cross-strategy LOSO (Table 12), downstream curation (Table 13), cross-scale 7B probe (Table 14), and wild-data calibration (Tables 15-16). This is unusually thorough for a 10-page paper.\n\n- **Comprehensive and fair baselines.** Nine methods spanning binary detector repurposing (DivEye, LOKI), ordinal regression (CORAL), standard classification (CE), feature-based (RF), cascade approaches (binary cascade), direct regression (PPL), end-to-end neural (DeBERTa E2E), and zero-shot prompting (GPT-4o). The inclusion of CORAL-DeBERTa with the same encoder backbone isolates DCOL's specific modifications from encoder choice.\n\n- **Generalization is rigorously tested.** Both leave-one-model-out (mean 47.0% +/- 3.6% on 6-class, 71.4% +/- 3.1% on 4-class) and leave-one-strategy-out (mean 52.0% +/- 2.1% on 6-class) experiments directly address the critical external validity question. The finding that strategy transfer is easier than model transfer (5pp gap vs ~10pp gap) is informative.\n\n- **Ablation cleanly decomposes contributions.** Full DCOL 57.1%, minus strategy conditioning 56.2% (-0.9), minus surprisal anchors 55.8% (-1.3), minus learnable margins 54.7% (-2.4), minus all three 53.1% (-4.0). Each component has an independent, monotonically ordered contribution. The sum of individual drops (4.6pp) slightly exceeds the joint drop (4.0pp), indicating mild positive interaction -- this is reported transparently.\n\n- **Extended depth analysis (Table 9) is scientifically valuable.** The monotonically decreasing AUC curve from 0v1 (0.998) through 5v6 (0.573) provides empirical grounding for the EDD concept and shows where the signal exhausts.\n\n- **Statistical significance is reported** for key comparisons (paired bootstrap for DCOL vs CORAL), and confidence intervals appear for several metrics. The graduated curation experiment reports p=0.038 for the PPL improvement.\n\n### Weaknesses (Methods)\n\n- **(Major) The contrastive loss formulation appears incomplete or incorrectly presented.** The N-pair contrastive loss as described -- L = sum max(0, ||h_i - h_j||_2 - m(|d_i - d_j|))^2 -- only penalizes when embedding distance exceeds the margin. This is a pull-only loss that is trivially minimized by collapsing all embeddings to a single point. A correct contrastive ordinal loss requires both an attractive term (for same/nearby depths) and a repulsive term (for distant depths), or an explicit decomposition into positive and negative pairs with different margin constraints. If the loss is correctly implemented but incorrectly described, this is a major reproducibility failure. If the loss is actually as written, the method is fundamentally flawed.\n\n- **(Major) Surprisal computation reference model creates potential circularity.** The surprisal-augmented anchors compute token-level surprisal under \"a reference model,\" but the paper does not specify: (a) which model serves as reference across the three model families, (b) whether the generating model is ever used as its own reference model (which would create a tautological signal for in-distribution evaluation), (c) how the reference model is chosen at inference on wild data where the generating model is unknown. The surprisal anchors contribute 1.3pp in ablation -- not negligible -- and this circularity concern directly impacts the claimed model-agnosticity of the approach.\n\n- **(Major) Depth-0 confound inflates headline numbers.** Depth 0 is human-written text (presumably Wikipedia or similar); depths 1-5 are model-generated. The d0-vs-d1 AUC of 0.998 is essentially binary human-vs-machine detection, a near-solved problem. The 6-class headline accuracy (57.1%) and 4-class accuracy (82.3%) are substantially boosted by near-perfect d0 classification. A 5-class accuracy (d1-d5 only) should be reported as a secondary headline metric to isolate the genuine ordinal depth estimation signal from the binary detection signal. Without this, the reader cannot assess how well DCOL actually distinguishes between different generations of synthetic text.\n\n- **(Minor) k-NN inference is underspecified.** The paper mentions an ordinal regression head for classification but the review context also references k-NN in the embedding space. The inference procedure, including any hyperparameters (k, distance metric), is not described with sufficient detail for reproduction. If results are sensitive to these choices, this is a hidden degree of freedom.\n\n- **(Minor) The margin monotonicity constraint implementation is not specified.** The paper states m_1 >= m_2 >= ... >= m_K > 0 but does not explain the enforcement mechanism (projected gradient? softmax reparameterization? cumulative sum of positive parameters?). Different implementations could lead to different optimization landscapes.\n\n- **(Minor) Strategy conditioning at inference on unknown-strategy data is unclear.** For wild-data deployment (Section 4.11, Tables 15-16), the decoding strategy is unknown. The paper does not explain how strategy conditioning is handled in this setting -- is a default/average strategy embedding used? Is conditioning disabled? This directly affects the practical applicability claimed in the downstream curation experiment.\n\n### Weaknesses (Experiments)\n\n- **(Major) The DCOL improvement over baselines is modest.** DCOL achieves 57.1% vs CORAL 53.8% (+3.3pp) and vs DeBERTa E2E 55.3% (+1.8pp). Given that DeBERTa E2E -- a straightforward fine-tuning baseline with no ordinal or contrastive structure -- already achieves 55.3%, the marginal value of DCOL's three text-specific modifications is narrow. The ablation confirms this: all three modifications together contribute only 4.0pp over the stripped-down contrastive framework (53.1%). The paper would benefit from honestly acknowledging that the task formalization and Atlas are the primary contributions, not the method.\n\n- **(Major) Cross-model generalization shows concerning failure cases.** OLMo held-out fails the 0.45 gate at 42.1% (6-class), while mean is 47.0%. A 42% accuracy on a held-out model family -- while above chance -- raises questions about practical deployment. The 4-class cross-model mean of 71.4% is more encouraging, but the high variance (+/- 3.1%) across only 3 folds means this confidence interval is unreliable. The gap between in-distribution (57.1% 6-class) and cross-model (47.0%) is 10pp, suggesting the model captures substantial model-specific rather than model-agnostic signal.\n\n- **(Minor) Cross-scale evaluation is too limited to support its claims.** The 7B probe (Table 14) uses only 4-class (depth 0-3 implied by zero-shot 61.4% and fine-tuned 79.1%), compared to the 6-class main setting. Only one larger model is tested. The claim that \"depth signals transfer across model scales\" rests on a single data point with reduced granularity.\n\n- **(Minor) Downstream curation experiment lacks task-based evaluation.** The 3.1% PPL reduction (18.14 vs 18.72) with p=0.038 is marginal in both statistical significance and practical magnitude. PPL on a held-out subset could reflect distributional matching rather than genuine quality improvement. No evaluation on downstream NLP tasks (MMLU, reasoning benchmarks, etc.) is provided. The oracle PPL (17.91) shows that even perfect depth knowledge yields only modest improvement, limiting the practical upside of this approach.\n\n- **(Minor) Wild-data calibration provides weak evidence.** Spearman rho of 0.341 (PPL) and 0.283 (AlignScore) are low correlations. Without ground-truth depth labels on wild data, these numbers are consistent with DCOL learning a noisy perplexity proxy rather than genuine depth estimation. The partial correlation analysis partially addresses this, but the evidence remains circumstantial.\n\n- **(Minor) No error analysis or confusion matrix.** The paper does not show a confusion matrix or analyze which types of text (topics, lengths, styles) are systematically misclassified. Understanding failure modes is important for a new task definition paper.\n\n- **(Minor) No figures in an empirical paper.** An Atlas paper with 16 tables and zero figures is a missed opportunity. Visualization of feature evolution trajectories, confusion matrices, embedding space structure, and AUC decay curves would substantially improve readability and scientific communication.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. Provide the complete training objective including the repulsive/floor term for the contrastive loss. As written, Eq. 1 is trivially minimized by embedding collapse.\n\n2. Which reference model computes token-level surprisal? Is it the same model that generated the text? How is the reference model chosen at inference on wild data?\n\n3. What is the 5-class (d1-d5 only) accuracy for DCOL and all baselines?\n\n4. How is the monotonicity constraint on margins enforced during training?\n\n5. How does strategy conditioning operate at inference when the decoding strategy is unknown (wild data, deployment)?\n\n6. Has depth-0 been tested with non-Wikipedia seed corpora to verify the signal is not a domain artifact?\n\n7. For the cross-model generalization, are confidence intervals computed via bootstrap over the single held-out fold, or across the 3 LOO folds? The latter has only 3 data points, making CIs unreliable.\n\n8. Can you provide error bars for the anomalous d4-d5 AUC recovery in Pythia nucleus (Table 9)?\n\n9. For the graduated curation experiment, what happens when you match data volume -- binary filtering at a higher threshold to retain ~92% of data, same as graduated?\n\n### Missing Experiments or Analyses\n\n- **5-class (d1-d5) accuracy** to isolate ordinal depth estimation from binary human-vs-machine detection. This is the single most important missing metric.\n\n- **Volume-matched curation comparison** where binary filtering retains the same percentage of data as graduated weighting, to disentangle the effect of more data from the effect of graduated weighting.\n\n- **Task-based downstream evaluation** beyond perplexity (MMLU, HellaSwag, or similar) for the curation experiment.\n\n- **Confusion matrix visualization** showing the ordinal structure of errors.\n\n- **Reference model sensitivity analysis** for surprisal computation -- does performance change when using a different reference model?\n\n- **EDD threshold sensitivity** -- how does the EDD matrix change at thresholds of 0.70 and 0.80 instead of 0.75?\n\n### Preliminary Scores\n- Soundness: 3/5\n- Excitement: 3.5/5\n- Overall: 3/5\n- Confidence: 4/5\n\n**Rationale:** The task formulation is genuinely novel and timely, and the Depth Degradation Atlas is a meaningful scientific contribution to the model collapse literature. The experimental coverage is exceptionally thorough. However, the incomplete/incorrect loss formulation is a critical technical concern that undermines reproducibility. The modest improvement of DCOL over simple baselines (only +1.8pp over vanilla DeBERTa fine-tuning) weakens the methodological contribution. The depth-0 confound inflates headline numbers, and the surprisal circularity is unaddressed. If the authors clarify the loss function, report d1-d5 accuracy, and reposition DCOL as one strong method within a broader task-formalization contribution (rather than the headline), this paper would likely reach a solid accept. The Atlas and EDD alone justify publication at a top venue, but the current framing oversells the method and undersells the empirical characterization."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: ac9eeea2ab163ccfb (use SendMessage with to: 'ac9eeea2ab163ccfb' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 84851\ntool_uses: 20\nduration_ms: 151170</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4ea398ee-be27-4c61-8a35-22ee8aa61ddb","uuid":"a6194c2a-376f-4d5a-8908-bdbd4099df7d","timestamp":"2026-05-14T14:53:44.134Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an EMNLP 2026 paper review. Your focus areas are **Methods** and **Experiments**. \n\n## Venue Standards\nEMNLP 2026 uses ACL Rolling Review (ARR). Score scale:\n- 5 = Consider for Award (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Conference (accept to main conference)\n- 3.5 = Borderline Conference\n- 3 = Findings\n- 2.5 = Borderline Findings\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1.5 = Resubmit after next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\n\nDimensions: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Overall Assessment (1-5, with half-points allowed).\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title:** \"Ordinal Depth Estimation of Recursive Generation: A Depth Degradation Atlas for Model Collapse Characterization\"\n\nThis paper formalizes ordinal generation depth estimation — predicting how many recursive generation cycles (d ∈ {0,1,...,5}) a text has undergone. Three contributions:\n1. **DCOL** (Depth Contrastive Ordinal Learning): DeBERTa-v3-large + N-pair contrastive loss with learnable non-uniform margins, surprisal-augmented anchor selection, and strategy conditioning. Achieves 57.1% 6-class acc (chance=16.7%), 82.3% 4-class acc, outperforming best baseline (CORAL) by +3.3pp.\n2. **Depth Degradation Atlas**: Systematic characterization across 3 models (Pythia-1.4B, OLMo-1B, GPT-2 XL) × 3 decoding strategies × 6 depths. Key finding: Effective Discriminable Depth (EDD) ≈ 3. Surprisal variance is the most stable depth signal (monotonicity 0.847).\n3. **Depth-graduated curation proof-of-concept**: Graduated weighting reduces PPL by 3.1% over binary filtering (p=0.038).\n\n## Paper Details (from the 10-page PDF)\n\n### Method (Section 3):\n- Problem: estimate d ∈ {0,...,K} from text. Key property: inter-depth distances are non-uniform (d=0→1 gap >> d=2→3 gap).\n- Benchmark: 3 model families × 3 decoding strategies (nucleus p=0.95, temperature τ=0.9, top-k k=50) × 6 depths, ~1200 samples/depth/strategy.\n- 19 statistical features: surprisal stats (mean, var, p75, p90), perplexity stats (mean, p75, p90), lexical (Zipf α, TTR, hapax, repetition), distribution (entropy, KL div, tail mass).\n- DCOL: DeBERTa-v3-large encoder → N-pair contrastive loss L = Σ max(0, ||hi-hj||₂ - m(|di-dj|))². Three modifications: (a) learnable non-uniform margins, (b) surprisal-augmented anchor selection, (c) strategy conditioning via lightweight embedding.\n- Classification head: ordinal regression mapping to cumulative probabilities, predict d as largest d where P(D>d)>0.5.\n\n### Experiments (Sections 4.1–4.11):\n- **Per-model benchmarks** (Table 1): GPT-2 XL 79.8%, Pythia 78.2%, OLMo 75.4% (4-class, RF+features). AUC monotonically decreasing 0v1→2v3.\n- **Per-strategy** (Tables 2-3): Nucleus best, temperature worst.\n- **Full baseline comparison** (Table 4): 9 methods. DCOL 57.1% > CORAL 53.8% > DeBERTa E2E 55.3% > CE 51.9% > RF 49.7% > Binary cascade 46.1% > DivEye 43.2% > LOKI 40.8% > PPL regression 38.9% > Zero-shot GPT-4o 28.7%. Note: DeBERTa E2E (55.3%) actually outperforms CORAL (53.8%) — the Δ column shows +1.5pp for E2E relative to CORAL.\n- **E2E vs DCOL** (Table 5): DCOL 57.1%, E2E 55.3%, RF 49.7%.\n- **Ablation** (Table 6): Full 57.1%, −strategy conditioning 56.2% (−0.9), −surprisal anchors 55.8% (−1.3), −learnable margins 54.7% (−2.4), −all three 53.1% (−4.0).\n- **EDD table** (Table 7): Median=3, range 2-4. OLMo+temp=2, GPT2XL+nucleus=4.\n- **Feature monotonicity** (Table 8): var_surprisal 0.847, p90_ppl 0.823, mean_ppl 0.801, zipf_alpha 0.768, mean_surprisal 0.752.\n- **Extended depth** (Table 9): Pythia nucleus AUC: 0v1=.998, 1v2=.861, 2v3=.801, 3v4=.708, 4v5=.621, 5v6=.573.\n- **Granularity ablation** (Table 10): Binary 94.6%, 3-class 86.7%, 4-class 82.3%, 6-class 57.1%. Lift peaks at 4-class (+57.3pp).\n- **LOMO cross-model** (Table 11): Mean 47.0%±3.6% (6-class), 71.4%±3.1% (4-class). OLMo held-out fails 0.45 gate (42.1%).\n- **LOSO cross-strategy** (Table 12): Mean 52.0%±2.1% (6-class). Strategy transfer easier than model transfer.\n- **Curation PoC** (Table 13): Graduated 18.14 PPL vs binary 18.72, no filter 19.85, oracle 17.91.\n- **7B cross-scale** (Table 14): Zero-shot 61.4% (4-cl), fine-tuned 79.1%, in-dist 82.3%.\n- **Wild-data** (Tables 15-16): ρ=0.341 (PPL), ρ=0.283 (AlignScore). 72% depth-0, 6% depth-3+.\n\n### Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml):\nThe YAML lists 13-14 imagined experiments. The paper covers ALL of them across its 11 experiment subsections. Coverage is comprehensive.\n\n## Your Task\nWrite a detailed R1 review focusing on:\n1. **Soundness of the method** — Is the DCOL formulation technically sound? Is the loss function correct? Is the ordinal regression head appropriate?\n2. **Experimental rigor** — Are experiments sufficient? Are baselines fair and comprehensive? Are evaluation metrics appropriate? Statistical significance?\n3. **Ablation quality** — Does the ablation convincingly decompose DCOL's contributions?\n4. **Reproducibility** — Are enough details provided?\n5. **Weaknesses in methodology or experiments**\n\nStructure your review as:\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods & Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths (Methods)\n[Bulleted]\n\n### Strengths (Experiments)\n[Bulleted]\n\n### Weaknesses (Methods)\n[Bulleted, with severity: major/minor]\n\n### Weaknesses (Experiments)\n[Bulleted, with severity: major/minor]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered]\n\n### Missing Experiments or Analyses\n[If any from the context weren't adequately covered]\n\n### Preliminary Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Overall: X/5\n- Confidence: X/5\n```\n\nBe rigorous but fair. This is for EMNLP — a top NLP venue. Consider both the technical merit and the practical significance. Do NOT mention any tier information (best/median/worst). Evaluate the paper purely on its own merits.","agentId":"ac9eeea2ab163ccfb","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nNow I have all the context needed. Let me carefully analyze the discrepancies between the different number sets provided in the task prompt versus the paper.tex, and write a thorough R1 review based on the numbers explicitly given in the task prompt (which represent the paper being reviewed).\n\nKey observation: The task prompt numbers (57.1% DCOL 6-class, 82.3% 4-class, CORAL at 53.8%) are substantially lower than the \"best\" dream paper.tex numbers (72.2%, etc.). The task prompt also mentions LOKI and a \"binary cascade\" baseline not in the paper.tex, and references Tables up to Table 16. This appears to be a different (more realistic) version of the paper results. I will review the paper as described in the task prompt.\n\n## R1 Review: Methods & Experiments\n\n### Summary\nThis paper formalizes ordinal generation depth estimation -- predicting the number of recursive generation cycles (d in {0,...,5}) a text has undergone -- and proposes DCOL, a DeBERTa-v3-large method with contrastive ordinal loss incorporating learnable non-uniform margins, surprisal-augmented anchor selection, and strategy conditioning. The paper also contributes a Depth Degradation Atlas characterizing feature evolution across 3 models x 3 decoding strategies x 6 depths, and a proof-of-concept showing depth-graduated curation outperforms binary filtering. DCOL achieves 57.1% 6-class accuracy (chance 16.7%) and 82.3% 4-class accuracy, outperforming the best baseline (CORAL) by +3.3pp on 6-class. The experimental coverage is remarkably comprehensive, spanning 11 subsections with per-model benchmarks, generalization experiments, ablations, extended depth analysis, granularity studies, wild-data calibration, and downstream application.\n\n### Strengths (Methods)\n\n- **Well-motivated design loop.** The Atlas documents non-uniform inter-depth geometry (d0->1 gap is 2.83x the d2->3 gap), which directly motivates the learnable non-uniform margins in DCOL. The learned margin ratios closely mirror empirical distances (reported Pearson r > 0.95). This observation-to-design-to-validation chain is methodologically clean.\n\n- **Ordinal inductive bias is principled.** The ordinal regression head mapping to cumulative probabilities P(D>d) with threshold at 0.5 is a well-established approach (extending CORAL). The monotonicity constraint on margins (m_1 >= m_2 >= ... >= m_K > 0) is a sensible structural prior matching the documented decay in inter-depth distances.\n\n- **Feature engineering is well-grounded.** The 19-dimensional statistical feature vector covering surprisal statistics, perplexity statistics, lexical diversity, and distributional measures is comprehensive. The identification of surprisal variance as the strongest depth signal (monotonicity 0.847) has standalone scientific value and is theoretically motivated by the variance compression inherent in recursive sampling.\n\n- **Strategy conditioning addresses a real problem.** Different decoding strategies produce different depth boundary locations (EDD ranges from 2 for OLMo+temperature to 4 for GPT2XL+nucleus). The lightweight strategy embedding modulation is an appropriate solution.\n\n### Strengths (Experiments)\n\n- **Exceptional experimental breadth.** Eleven experiment subsections covering per-model benchmarks (Tables 1-3), full baseline comparison (Table 4), E2E comparison (Table 5), ablation (Table 6), EDD analysis (Table 7), feature monotonicity (Table 8), extended depth (Table 9), granularity (Table 10), cross-model LOMO (Table 11), cross-strategy LOSO (Table 12), downstream curation (Table 13), cross-scale 7B probe (Table 14), and wild-data calibration (Tables 15-16). This is unusually thorough for a 10-page paper.\n\n- **Comprehensive and fair baselines.** Nine methods spanning binary detector repurposing (DivEye, LOKI), ordinal regression (CORAL), standard classification (CE), feature-based (RF), cascade approaches (binary cascade), direct regression (PPL), end-to-end neural (DeBERTa E2E), and zero-shot prompting (GPT-4o). The inclusion of CORAL-DeBERTa with the same encoder backbone isolates DCOL's specific modifications from encoder choice.\n\n- **Generalization is rigorously tested.** Both leave-one-model-out (mean 47.0% +/- 3.6% on 6-class, 71.4% +/- 3.1% on 4-class) and leave-one-strategy-out (mean 52.0% +/- 2.1% on 6-class) experiments directly address the critical external validity question. The finding that strategy transfer is easier than model transfer (5pp gap vs ~10pp gap) is informative.\n\n- **Ablation cleanly decomposes contributions.** Full DCOL 57.1%, minus strategy conditioning 56.2% (-0.9), minus surprisal anchors 55.8% (-1.3), minus learnable margins 54.7% (-2.4), minus all three 53.1% (-4.0). Each component has an independent, monotonically ordered contribution. The sum of individual drops (4.6pp) slightly exceeds the joint drop (4.0pp), indicating mild positive interaction -- this is reported transparently.\n\n- **Extended depth analysis (Table 9) is scientifically valuable.** The monotonically decreasing AUC curve from 0v1 (0.998) through 5v6 (0.573) provides empirical grounding for the EDD concept and shows where the signal exhausts.\n\n- **Statistical significance is reported** for key comparisons (paired bootstrap for DCOL vs CORAL), and confidence intervals appear for several metrics. The graduated curation experiment reports p=0.038 for the PPL improvement.\n\n### Weaknesses (Methods)\n\n- **(Major) The contrastive loss formulation appears incomplete or incorrectly presented.** The N-pair contrastive loss as described -- L = sum max(0, ||h_i - h_j||_2 - m(|d_i - d_j|))^2 -- only penalizes when embedding distance exceeds the margin. This is a pull-only loss that is trivially minimized by collapsing all embeddings to a single point. A correct contrastive ordinal loss requires both an attractive term (for same/nearby depths) and a repulsive term (for distant depths), or an explicit decomposition into positive and negative pairs with different margin constraints. If the loss is correctly implemented but incorrectly described, this is a major reproducibility failure. If the loss is actually as written, the method is fundamentally flawed.\n\n- **(Major) Surprisal computation reference model creates potential circularity.** The surprisal-augmented anchors compute token-level surprisal under \"a reference model,\" but the paper does not specify: (a) which model serves as reference across the three model families, (b) whether the generating model is ever used as its own reference model (which would create a tautological signal for in-distribution evaluation), (c) how the reference model is chosen at inference on wild data where the generating model is unknown. The surprisal anchors contribute 1.3pp in ablation -- not negligible -- and this circularity concern directly impacts the claimed model-agnosticity of the approach.\n\n- **(Major) Depth-0 confound inflates headline numbers.** Depth 0 is human-written text (presumably Wikipedia or similar); depths 1-5 are model-generated. The d0-vs-d1 AUC of 0.998 is essentially binary human-vs-machine detection, a near-solved problem. The 6-class headline accuracy (57.1%) and 4-class accuracy (82.3%) are substantially boosted by near-perfect d0 classification. A 5-class accuracy (d1-d5 only) should be reported as a secondary headline metric to isolate the genuine ordinal depth estimation signal from the binary detection signal. Without this, the reader cannot assess how well DCOL actually distinguishes between different generations of synthetic text.\n\n- **(Minor) k-NN inference is underspecified.** The paper mentions an ordinal regression head for classification but the review context also references k-NN in the embedding space. The inference procedure, including any hyperparameters (k, distance metric), is not described with sufficient detail for reproduction. If results are sensitive to these choices, this is a hidden degree of freedom.\n\n- **(Minor) The margin monotonicity constraint implementation is not specified.** The paper states m_1 >= m_2 >= ... >= m_K > 0 but does not explain the enforcement mechanism (projected gradient? softmax reparameterization? cumulative sum of positive parameters?). Different implementations could lead to different optimization landscapes.\n\n- **(Minor) Strategy conditioning at inference on unknown-strategy data is unclear.** For wild-data deployment (Section 4.11, Tables 15-16), the decoding strategy is unknown. The paper does not explain how strategy conditioning is handled in this setting -- is a default/average strategy embedding used? Is conditioning disabled? This directly affects the practical applicability claimed in the downstream curation experiment.\n\n### Weaknesses (Experiments)\n\n- **(Major) The DCOL improvement over baselines is modest.** DCOL achieves 57.1% vs CORAL 53.8% (+3.3pp) and vs DeBERTa E2E 55.3% (+1.8pp). Given that DeBERTa E2E -- a straightforward fine-tuning baseline with no ordinal or contrastive structure -- already achieves 55.3%, the marginal value of DCOL's three text-specific modifications is narrow. The ablation confirms this: all three modifications together contribute only 4.0pp over the stripped-down contrastive framework (53.1%). The paper would benefit from honestly acknowledging that the task formalization and Atlas are the primary contributions, not the method.\n\n- **(Major) Cross-model generalization shows concerning failure cases.** OLMo held-out fails the 0.45 gate at 42.1% (6-class), while mean is 47.0%. A 42% accuracy on a held-out model family -- while above chance -- raises questions about practical deployment. The 4-class cross-model mean of 71.4% is more encouraging, but the high variance (+/- 3.1%) across only 3 folds means this confidence interval is unreliable. The gap between in-distribution (57.1% 6-class) and cross-model (47.0%) is 10pp, suggesting the model captures substantial model-specific rather than model-agnostic signal.\n\n- **(Minor) Cross-scale evaluation is too limited to support its claims.** The 7B probe (Table 14) uses only 4-class (depth 0-3 implied by zero-shot 61.4% and fine-tuned 79.1%), compared to the 6-class main setting. Only one larger model is tested. The claim that \"depth signals transfer across model scales\" rests on a single data point with reduced granularity.\n\n- **(Minor) Downstream curation experiment lacks task-based evaluation.** The 3.1% PPL reduction (18.14 vs 18.72) with p=0.038 is marginal in both statistical significance and practical magnitude. PPL on a held-out subset could reflect distributional matching rather than genuine quality improvement. No evaluation on downstream NLP tasks (MMLU, reasoning benchmarks, etc.) is provided. The oracle PPL (17.91) shows that even perfect depth knowledge yields only modest improvement, limiting the practical upside of this approach.\n\n- **(Minor) Wild-data calibration provides weak evidence.** Spearman rho of 0.341 (PPL) and 0.283 (AlignScore) are low correlations. Without ground-truth depth labels on wild data, these numbers are consistent with DCOL learning a noisy perplexity proxy rather than genuine depth estimation. The partial correlation analysis partially addresses this, but the evidence remains circumstantial.\n\n- **(Minor) No error analysis or confusion matrix.** The paper does not show a confusion matrix or analyze which types of text (topics, lengths, styles) are systematically misclassified. Understanding failure modes is important for a new task definition paper.\n\n- **(Minor) No figures in an empirical paper.** An Atlas paper with 16 tables and zero figures is a missed opportunity. Visualization of feature evolution trajectories, confusion matrices, embedding space structure, and AUC decay curves would substantially improve readability and scientific communication.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. Provide the complete training objective including the repulsive/floor term for the contrastive loss. As written, Eq. 1 is trivially minimized by embedding collapse.\n\n2. Which reference model computes token-level surprisal? Is it the same model that generated the text? How is the reference model chosen at inference on wild data?\n\n3. What is the 5-class (d1-d5 only) accuracy for DCOL and all baselines?\n\n4. How is the monotonicity constraint on margins enforced during training?\n\n5. How does strategy conditioning operate at inference when the decoding strategy is unknown (wild data, deployment)?\n\n6. Has depth-0 been tested with non-Wikipedia seed corpora to verify the signal is not a domain artifact?\n\n7. For the cross-model generalization, are confidence intervals computed via bootstrap over the single held-out fold, or across the 3 LOO folds? The latter has only 3 data points, making CIs unreliable.\n\n8. Can you provide error bars for the anomalous d4-d5 AUC recovery in Pythia nucleus (Table 9)?\n\n9. For the graduated curation experiment, what happens when you match data volume -- binary filtering at a higher threshold to retain ~92% of data, same as graduated?\n\n### Missing Experiments or Analyses\n\n- **5-class (d1-d5) accuracy** to isolate ordinal depth estimation from binary human-vs-machine detection. This is the single most important missing metric.\n\n- **Volume-matched curation comparison** where binary filtering retains the same percentage of data as graduated weighting, to disentangle the effect of more data from the effect of graduated weighting.\n\n- **Task-based downstream evaluation** beyond perplexity (MMLU, HellaSwag, or similar) for the curation experiment.\n\n- **Confusion matrix visualization** showing the ordinal structure of errors.\n\n- **Reference model sensitivity analysis** for surprisal computation -- does performance change when using a different reference model?\n\n- **EDD threshold sensitivity** -- how does the EDD matrix change at thresholds of 0.70 and 0.80 instead of 0.75?\n\n### Preliminary Scores\n- Soundness: 3/5\n- Excitement: 3.5/5\n- Overall: 3/5\n- Confidence: 4/5\n\n**Rationale:** The task formulation is genuinely novel and timely, and the Depth Degradation Atlas is a meaningful scientific contribution to the model collapse literature. The experimental coverage is exceptionally thorough. However, the incomplete/incorrect loss formulation is a critical technical concern that undermines reproducibility. The modest improvement of DCOL over simple baselines (only +1.8pp over vanilla DeBERTa fine-tuning) weakens the methodological contribution. The depth-0 confound inflates headline numbers, and the surprisal circularity is unaddressed. If the authors clarify the loss function, report d1-d5 accuracy, and reposition DCOL as one strong method within a broader task-formalization contribution (rather than the headline), this paper would likely reach a solid accept. The Atlas and EDD alone justify publication at a top venue, but the current framing oversells the method and undersells the empirical characterization."}],"totalDurationMs":151170,"totalTokens":84851,"totalToolUseCount":20,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":742,"cache_read_input_tokens":80370,"output_tokens":3736,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":742},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":3736,"cache_read_input_tokens":80370,"cache_creation_input_tokens":742,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":742,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":4,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":13}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a4d26d750abf8bf19","tool_use_id":"toolu_01CDn82zQJAFdMpsQiadmVkm","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","usage":{"total_tokens":61642,"tool_uses":17,"duration_ms":154119},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"0da90b72-d4c9-47da-88da-d44359738edd","session_id":"4ea398ee-be27-4c61-8a35-22ee8aa61ddb"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01QL4MVhwJyaSSzXTrfZ3fSU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01ESgFdUHnMzC7Z4aL16ggQL","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"project":"recursive_gen_depth_est","tier":"median","dream_id":"dream_20260514T140607","overall_score":"borderline findings","report":"---\ndream_id: dream_20260514T140607\ntier: median\noverall_score: borderline findings\ntimestamp: 2026-05-14T15:30:00+00:00\n---\n\n\n# Dream Review Report: Ordinal Depth Estimation of Recursive Generation: A Depth Degradation Atlas for Model Collapse Characterization\n\n**Venue:** EMNLP 2026 (ACL Rolling Review)\n**Review Mode:** R2 — Novelty & Related Work\n**Date:** 2026-05-14\n\n---\n\n## R2 Review: Novelty & Related Work\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper formalizes ordinal generation depth estimation -- predicting how many recursive model-to-data-to-model cycles a text has undergone -- and proposes DCOL, a DeBERTa-based contrastive ordinal classifier with learnable non-uniform margins. Alongside the classifier, the paper contributes a Depth Degradation Atlas documenting how statistical features evolve across depths, models, and strategies, establishing an Effective Discriminable Depth (EDD) of approximately 3. The task formulation is genuinely novel and timely, and the Atlas provides useful empirical characterization. However, the DCOL method is a straightforward adaptation of existing contrastive ordinal learning with modest gains (+3.3pp over CORAL), the related work section is critically sparse (only 3 references in the bibliography), and certain claims exceed what the evidence supports.\n\n### Strengths (Novelty)\n\n- **Legitimate task novelty.** Ordinal depth estimation is a well-defined new problem. The insight that binary detection (real vs. synthetic) discards actionable ordinal information about recursive contamination severity is simple but important. No prior work has formalized this task, and the problem is well-motivated by the model collapse literature.\n\n- **EDD is a useful conceptual contribution.** Defining the Effective Discriminable Depth as the boundary beyond which adjacent depths become indistinguishable provides a concrete, quantitative answer to a previously unasked question. The finding that EDD varies across model-strategy combinations (range 2-4, median 3) is itself informative -- it shows this boundary is not universal but depends on model capability and decoding strategy.\n\n- **Atlas goes beyond prior characterization work.** Drayson et al. (2025) characterized non-monotonic degradation for individual settings. This paper systematically maps 19 features across 9 model-strategy combinations and 6 depths, yielding the \"feature handoff\" insight (surprisal variance dominates within EDD, Zipf alpha beyond) and documenting non-uniform inter-depth distances. This extends the empirical landscape meaningfully.\n\n- **Graduated curation as a conceptual paradigm.** Even at proof-of-concept scale, the idea that predicted depth can serve as a continuous quality proxy for data curation -- retaining useful shallow-generation content while down-weighting deeply recursive text -- is a useful contribution to the data curation discourse.\n\n### Strengths (Presentation & Framing)\n\n- **Appropriately scoped limitations section.** The paper is commendably honest about its weaknesses: the OLMo cross-model failure, effective granularity limited to 4 classes, scale transfer gap, controlled-vs-wild gap, and the moderate DCOL advantage. This calibrated self-assessment builds trust.\n\n- **Granularity analysis is well-designed.** Reporting accuracy across binary / 3-class / 4-class / 6-class granularities with lift-over-chance analysis gives a clear picture of the information-feasibility tradeoff. The finding that lift peaks at 4-class (+57.3pp) directly supports the EDD narrative.\n\n- **Clear experimental progression.** The paper proceeds logically from per-model validation to pooled comparisons to generalization to downstream application. Each experiment answers a natural follow-up question from the preceding one.\n\n### Weaknesses (Novelty)\n\n- **(Major) DCOL is an incremental adaptation of CLOC with minimal methodological novelty.** The base contrastive ordinal framework is taken directly from CLOC (CVPR 2025), an age estimation method. The three \"text-specific modifications\" -- learnable non-uniform margins, surprisal-augmented anchor selection, and strategy conditioning -- are individually standard techniques in metric learning. Learnable margins appear in Wu et al. (2017) and Roth et al. (2020). Anchor selection guided by auxiliary features is a standard hard-negative mining strategy. Strategy conditioning is lightweight FiLM-style modulation. Each contributes only 0.9-2.4pp. The paper should explicitly acknowledge DCOL as \"CLOC adapted for text depth estimation\" rather than presenting it as a novel framework. The current framing overstates the methodological contribution.\n\n- **(Major) The DCOL advantage over strong baselines is modest and partially attributable to ordinal structure itself, not DCOL-specific innovations.** DCOL achieves 57.1% vs. CORAL's 53.8% (+3.3pp) and DeBERTa E2E's 55.3% (+1.8pp). The ablation reveals that removing all three text-specific modifications drops accuracy to 53.1% -- below CORAL (53.8%). This means the vanilla contrastive framework without DCOL's modifications actually underperforms CORAL's rank loss. The +3.3pp gain over CORAL is the sum of three engineering choices, not a fundamental methodological advance. At EMNLP, a +3.3pp improvement with a confidence interval that potentially overlaps the baseline warrants caution in how the method contribution is framed.\n\n- **(Minor) The benchmark construction, while necessary, follows a standard recipe.** Generating recursive chains by prompting models with their own output and extracting statistical features is methodologically straightforward. The benchmark is a necessary scaffold for the research question but is not itself a novel methodological contribution -- it is competent execution of an obvious approach once the task is defined.\n\n### Weaknesses (Related Work)\n\n- **(Major) The bibliography contains only 3 references -- this is critically inadequate for EMNLP.** A 10-page paper at a top venue should cite 30-50 references to properly position its contributions. The current related work section is approximately half a page with three paragraphs, each citing 1-3 works. Entire relevant subfields are unrepresented:\n\n  - **Watermarking-based provenance:** Kirchenbauer et al. (2023, \"A Watermark for Large Language Models\") is a glaring omission. Watermarking enables proactive provenance tracking of generation chains -- a direct alternative to post-hoc depth estimation. The paper should discuss why post-hoc estimation is needed when watermarking exists (answer: watermarks require generator cooperation, which is absent in uncontrolled feedback loops).\n\n  - **Data quality and selection literature:** DSIR (Xie et al., 2023), QuRating (Wettig et al., 2024), and the broader data-centric AI literature are directly relevant to the graduated curation contribution. The paper claims depth-graduated weighting outperforms binary filtering but does not position this against existing quality-aware data selection methods.\n\n  - **Multi-class synthetic text attribution:** Uchendu et al. (2020, \"Authorship Attribution for Neural Text Generation\") and follow-up work on attributing text to specific generators represent a precursor to multi-class synthetic text analysis. While attribution and depth estimation are different tasks, the machine learning techniques overlap substantially.\n\n  - **Model collapse theory beyond Shumailov:** Alemohammad et al. (2023, \"Self-Consuming Generative Models Go MAD\"), Briesch et al. (2023), and Guo et al. (2024, on linguistic diversity decline) are all relevant to the theoretical grounding of why depth estimation matters. The paper cites only Shumailov et al. and Drayson et al. for the entire model collapse literature.\n\n  - **Binary detection methods:** The paper mentions LOKI, DivEye, and Binoculars but does not cite them in the bibliography. Fast-DetectGPT (Bao et al., 2024) and DetectGPT (Mitchell et al., 2023) should be properly cited and ideally included as baselines (or their features used as baselines, as partially done).\n\n  - **Ordinal classification beyond CORAL:** The ordinal learning literature includes SORD (Diaz & Marathe, 2019), unimodal regularization (Beckham & Pal, 2017), and stick-breaking approaches. Citing only CORAL and CLOC gives an incomplete picture of the ordinal classification landscape.\n\n- **(Major) Positioning against binary detection is incomplete.** The paper claims to extend \"from binary to ordinal\" but does not engage substantively with what binary detectors actually measure. Many binary detectors use the same features this paper relies on (surprisal statistics, perplexity). The paper should clarify whether ordinal depth estimation is genuinely a new signal or whether it is ordinal regression on features already known to correlate with \"synthetic-ness.\" The d0-vs-d1 AUC of 0.998 suggests that at the coarsest level, the paper is recovering the same signal as binary detectors.\n\n- **(Minor) EditLens is mentioned but not cited.** The bibliography lacks a reference for EditLens, LOKI, DivEye, and Binoculars. Mentioning methods without proper citations is a significant formatting issue for a venue submission.\n\n### Weaknesses (Claims & Framing)\n\n- **(Major) The d0 confound inflates headline accuracy.** Depth 0 is human-written text; depth 1+ is model-generated. The d0-vs-d1 AUC of 0.998 indicates this boundary is essentially binary human-vs-machine detection, a solved problem. The 6-class accuracy of 57.1% and 4-class accuracy of 82.3% are substantially boosted by near-perfect d0 classification. The paper should report a d1-d5 (5-class, synthetic-only) accuracy as a complementary metric to show how well the method discriminates among different generations of synthetic text, which is the genuinely novel aspect of the task. Without this, the headline numbers conflate \"detecting synthetic text\" (well-studied) with \"estimating recursive depth\" (novel).\n\n- **(Major) \"First systematic empirical map\" claim needs qualification.** The Atlas covers 3 models, all in the 1-1.5B parameter range, and 3 decoding strategies. This is a reasonable starting point but calling it \"first systematic empirical map of recursive degradation\" overstates the coverage. Models of different architecture families (encoder-decoder, mixture-of-experts), different scales (7B+, 70B+), and different modalities are not represented. The claim should be scoped: \"the first systematic characterization at the 1B scale across three autoregressive model families.\"\n\n- **(Minor) The cross-model generalization framing buries the negative result.** The paper reports \"mean 47.0% 6-class\" but the OLMo rotation fails at 42.1% and does not pass the self-defined 0.45 gate. One out of three rotations failing a self-defined quality gate is a substantial negative result, not a minor caveat. The paper acknowledges this in limitations but the abstract and introduction present cross-model generalization more optimistically than warranted. Stating \"cross-model leave-one-out generalization averages 47.0%\" without immediately noting \"with one of three rotations failing\" in the abstract is selectively optimistic.\n\n- **(Minor) Downstream curation effect size is marginal.** The 3.1% PPL reduction (p=0.038) over binary filtering is barely significant. The confidence intervals for graduated vs. binary weighting likely overlap. Furthermore, the comparison may be confounded by data volume: binary filtering retains less data than graduated weighting, so the PPL improvement could partly reflect having more training data rather than better weighting. The paper appropriately scopes this as a proof-of-concept, but the abstract presents it as a concrete contribution rather than a preliminary finding.\n\n- **(Minor) EDD threshold is arbitrary and sensitivity is not analyzed.** EDD is defined as the maximum depth where adjacent-pair AUC >= 0.72. This threshold is not motivated. If it were 0.75, some combinations would drop from EDD=3 to EDD=2. If it were 0.70, some would increase. A sensitivity analysis showing EDD as a function of the threshold (e.g., a simple table or curve) would strengthen the claim that EDD is approximately 3 \"robustly.\"\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. How does DCOL differ from CLOC beyond the three listed modifications? Is the base contrastive loss formulation, the mining strategy, and the ordinal head identical to CLOC?\n\n2. Which reference model is used to compute surprisal features across all three model families? If Pythia-1.4B is used as the reference model for Pythia-generated text, this creates a circularity where the generating model's own surprisal on its output conflates model identity with depth signal. How does this affect the cross-model results?\n\n3. What is the 5-class (d1-d5) accuracy, excluding depth 0? This would isolate the genuinely novel \"ordinal depth discrimination\" signal from the well-studied \"human vs. machine\" signal.\n\n4. How does EDD change if the threshold is set to 0.70 or 0.75 instead of 0.72?\n\n5. Has the graduated curation experiment been compared against a volume-matched binary filtering baseline (i.e., binary filtering with a softer threshold that retains the same percentage of data as graduated weighting)?\n\n6. How does DCOL handle text generated by unknown strategies at inference time? The strategy conditioning module requires a strategy label -- what happens when this is unavailable, as would be the case for any real-world deployment?\n\n7. The paper mentions 19 statistical features but only discusses 5 in the monotonicity analysis. Are the remaining 14 features uninformative? Could a more compact feature set achieve similar performance?\n\n8. Why are there only 3 references in the bibliography? Is this an artifact of the submission format, or does the paper genuinely not cite works beyond Shumailov, Drayson, and Cao?\n\n### Missing References (non-exhaustive)\n\nThe following are works that should be cited and discussed:\n- Kirchenbauer et al. (2023) -- watermarking for LLM outputs (provenance alternative)\n- Mitchell et al. (2023) -- DetectGPT (binary detection baseline)\n- Bao et al. (2024) -- Fast-DetectGPT (binary detection baseline)\n- Alemohammad et al. (2023) -- self-consuming generative models (model collapse theory)\n- Briesch et al. (2023) -- LLMs suffering from own output (model collapse)\n- Guo et al. (2024) -- linguistic diversity decline in recursive generations\n- Uchendu et al. (2020) -- multi-class authorship attribution for generators\n- Xie et al. (2023) -- DSIR data selection (curation baseline)\n- Wettig et al. (2024) -- QuRating data quality (curation baseline)\n- Diaz & Marathe (2019) -- SORD soft ordinal regression\n- Beckham & Pal (2017) -- unimodal regularization for ordinal classification\n- Wu et al. (2017) -- learnable margins in metric learning\n- Roth et al. (2020) -- revisiting training strategies for metric learning\n\n### Preliminary Scores\n\n- Soundness: 3/5\n- Excitement: 2.5/5\n- Overall: 2.5/5\n- Confidence: 4/5\n\n### Score Justification\n\n**Soundness (3/5):** The experimental design is thorough -- per-model benchmarks, cross-model/strategy generalization, ablation, downstream application, and wild-data calibration form a comprehensive evaluation. However, the loss formulation in Eq. 1 appears to only include a pull/ceiling term with no repulsive mechanism (collapsing all embeddings to a point trivially satisfies it), which is either a critical omission in the writing or a genuine flaw. The d0 confound inflates headline numbers. These issues prevent a higher soundness score despite strong experimental coverage.\n\n**Excitement (2.5/5):** The task formulation is timely and the Atlas is useful, but the execution is incremental. DCOL's +3.3pp over CORAL is modest. The effective task granularity is limited to 4 classes. Cross-model generalization is unreliable (1/3 rotations fail). Wild-data correlations are weak (rho <= 0.34). The practical impact pathway -- from controlled benchmark to real-world data curation -- has several unsolved gaps. For the median tier of results, the excitement is dampened by the limited margin of improvement and constrained generalization.\n\n**Overall (2.5/5):** The paper sits at the borderline between Findings and main conference. The task definition and Atlas are genuine contributions that advance understanding of recursive contamination. However, the critically sparse related work (3 references), incremental methodology, inflated headline metrics (d0 confound), and modest effect sizes collectively place this below the main conference threshold. With a substantially expanded related work section, honest d1-d5 metrics, and repositioning of DCOL as adapted-CLOC, this could reach Findings quality. The gap to main conference acceptance requires either stronger empirical results (larger cross-model/wild-data performance) or deeper methodological novelty.\n\n**Confidence (4/5):** I am familiar with the synthetic text detection, model collapse, ordinal classification, and contrastive learning literatures. The related work gaps identified are based on direct knowledge of the cited and missing works.\n\n---\n\n## Comparison with Best-Tier Review Context\n\nThe median-tier paper shares the same structure and claims as the best tier but with weaker empirical results in several dimensions:\n- DCOL's advantage over CORAL is +3.3pp (same as best tier's reported number, but in the best tier the ablation showed larger per-component contributions and vanilla-contrastive still outperformed CORAL)\n- Cross-model mean is 47.0% with OLMo failing the gate (best tier reported 60.1% mean or similar, with all rotations passing)\n- Wild-data correlations are moderate (rho <= 0.34)\n- The 3-reference bibliography is identical across tiers, confirming this is a structural issue with the paper rather than a tier-specific concern\n\nThe core weakness pattern is consistent: the task and Atlas are solid contributions, but the method is incremental, the related work is critically underdeveloped, and the headline claims need better calibration to the actual evidence.\n\n---\n\n## Recommendation Summary\n\n**For revision (priority-ordered):**\n1. Expand related work from 3 to 25+ references, covering watermarking, data curation, multi-class attribution, model collapse theory, and ordinal classification\n2. Report d1-d5 (synthetic-only) accuracy as a secondary headline metric\n3. Reposition DCOL explicitly as \"CLOC adapted for text depth estimation\" -- the novelty is the task + Atlas\n4. Provide EDD threshold sensitivity analysis\n5. Clarify the complete loss function (is there a repulsive term?)\n6. Clarify the reference model for surprisal computation and address potential circularity\n7. Compare graduated vs. binary curation with matched data volume\n8. Add figures -- the paper is entirely tables, which is unusual and reduces accessibility"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1472,"cache_read_input_tokens":59916,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1472,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01CDn82zQJAFdMpsQiadmVkm","session_id":"4ea398ee-be27-4c61-8a35-22ee8aa61ddb","uuid":"404f59ba-c843-4ba6-9933-5cbe2c771dbf"}
